      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are right to be nervous, so stick to buying an over-the-counter Oral Rehydration Solution (like Pedialyte) instead of trying to make a salt-sugar mix to avoid dangerous electrolyte imbalances. If you must use plain water and homemade substitutes, offering small, frequent sips of **1 to 2 ounces** (about 4-6 teaspoons) every 15 to 20 minutes is generally safer than large amounts at once. Read the label on any replacement product carefully, as specific manufacturers often have different dosing recommendations for toddlers compared to adults. If he refuses to drink even these small amounts for more than a few hours, or if he shows signs of extreme lethargy or inability to urinate, do not hesitate to call your pediatrician or go to the ER immediately.",
